// wait_completion -- not an operation in its own right; the shared wait point
// of every end-to-end operation on this channel (operation model §3.1).
//
// Blocking is realised as
//
//     repeat { check_cond; yield }
//
// where check_cond is ordinary register-access code. A producer (interrupt
// observer, bus monitor, or the environment) causes blocked threads to
// re-evaluate; waiting threads wake and re-test their own conditions. Nothing
// is edge-triggered, so nothing can be missed: the condition is re-read from
// the device on every wake, and a spurious wake is harmless.
//
// THE SIDE-EFFECT PROBLEM, AND WHY THIS SHAPE (§3.1.1)
//
// check_cond runs an unbounded number of times, so it must not consume the
// state it is testing -- and on this device the obvious conditions do:
//
//   * Reading CHn_CSR CLEARS ERR (Table 6 "12 ROC", wb_dma_ch_rf.v:346) and
//     the three interrupt-source bits.
//   * BUSY means "this channel is the one the arbiter is currently servicing",
//     not "this channel is armed". An armed channel awaiting its turn reads
//     BUSY = 0, so !busy would report completion for a transfer that has not
//     started.
//   * INT_SRC_A/B is the one side-effect-free condition, but it is ANDed with
//     the routing mask, so an unrouted channel never appears in it.
//
// This model polls CHn_CSR anyway, per the review decision that operations are
// per-channel and a waking thread reads its own channel's status register. The
// clearing read is SAFE HERE because the value is captured in the same read
// that observes it: the loop exits with `csr` in hand, so consuming DONE/ERR
// costs nothing.
//
// It is safe only under the accompanying rule: AT MOST ONE OPERATION PER
// CHANNEL AT A TIME. Two threads polling the same channel would race to
// consume each other's completion. The one deliberate exception is
// stop_channel(), which is documented at its own call site.
import wb_dma_regs_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_types_pkg::*;

extend component wb_dma_ch_ops_c {

    target function wb_dma_status_e wait_completion() {
        wb_dma_ch_csr_s csr;

        while (true) {
            csr = regs.csr.read();
            if (csr.done == 1 || csr.err == 1) {
                break;
            }
            // Not ours yet -- hand the executor to whatever else is running.
            // With CHUNK_DONE enabled the interrupt also fires on events that
            // do NOT complete the operation, which is exactly why this is a
            // wake-and-recheck loop rather than a single suspend.
            yield;
        }

        if (csr.err == 1) {
            return WB_DMA_ERROR;
        }
        return WB_DMA_DONE;
    }
}
